      "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nLet <MATH_0001> be a group and define a site <MATH_0002> as follows: the underlying\ncategory is the category of <MATH_0003>-sets, i.e., its objects are sets endowed\nwith a left <MATH_0004>-action and the morphisms are equivariant maps; and the\ncoverings of <MATH_0005> are the families\n<MATH_0006> satisfying\n<MATH_0007>.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nThere is a special object in the site <MATH_0008>, namely the <MATH_0009>-set <MATH_0010>\nendowed with its natural action by left translations. We denote it <MATH_0011>.\nObserve that there is a natural group isomorphism\n<MATH_0012>\nIn particular, for any presheaf <MATH_0013>, the set <MATH_0014>\ninherits a <MATH_0015>-action via <MATH_0016>. (Note that by contravariance of\n<MATH_0017>, the set <MATH_0018> is again a left <MATH_0019>-set.) In fact,\nthe functor\n<MATH_0020>\nis an equivalence of categories. Its quasi-inverse is the functor <MATH_0021>. Without giving the complete proof (which can be found in\nSites, Section <REF_0001>)\nlet us try to explain why this is true.\n<STRUCT_0004>\n<STRUCT_0005>\nIf <MATH_0022> is a <MATH_0023>-set, we can decompose it into orbits <MATH_0024>. The sheaf axiom for the covering <MATH_0025> says that\n<MATH_0026>\nis an equalizer. Observing that fibered products in <MATH_0027> are\ninduced from fibered products in <MATH_0028>, and using the fact that\n<MATH_0029> is a <MATH_0030>-singleton, we get that\n<MATH_0031>\nand the two maps above are in fact the same. Therefore the sheaf axiom merely\nsays that <MATH_0032>.\n<STRUCT_0006>\nIf <MATH_0033> is the <MATH_0034>-set <MATH_0035> and <MATH_0036> is a sheaf on <MATH_0037>,\nthen we claim that\n<MATH_0038>\nand in particular <MATH_0039>. To see this,\nlet's use the sheaf axiom for the covering <MATH_0040> of <MATH_0041>. We\nhave\n<MATH_0042>\nis a disjoint union of copies of <MATH_0043> (as a <MATH_0044>-set). Hence the sheaf axiom\nreads\n<MATH_0045>\nwhere the two maps on the right are <MATH_0046> and <MATH_0047>. Therefore <MATH_0048> as\nclaimed.\n<STRUCT_0007>\nThis doesn't quite prove the claimed equivalence of categories, but it shows at\nleast that a sheaf <MATH_0049> is entirely determined by its sections over\n<MATH_0050>. Details (and set theoretical remarks) can be found in\nSites, Section <REF_0002>.\n\n\n\n\n",
